\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Cost-accuracy Pareto frontier across the three datasets (M5, Rohlik~v2, Favorita top-30k) on consumer hardware. X-axis: $\log _{10}$ USD of the full 9-job equivalent sweep per dataset per family, priced at the \emph  {marginal electricity} of an M-series laptop: ${\sim }30$\,W sustained draw under inference $\times $ Polish residential electricity (\$0.20/kWh) $\approx $ \$0.006/hr. This is the cost the practitioner actually pays on top of a machine they already own for other work; it excludes the sunk capex of the laptop itself. The hardware bucket \texttt  {M\_SERIES\_MAC} in \texttt  {benchmark/code/evaluation/cost.py} uses exactly these constants so that local-run rows on this figure are generated from the same cost model as the \textsc  {lgbm}{} re-costing below. Y-axis: the per-dataset \textsc  {wape}{}\,/\,\textsc  {wrmsse}{} of the best variant.}}{43}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pareto_hw}{{4}{43}{Cost-accuracy Pareto frontier across the three datasets (M5, Rohlik~v2, Favorita top-30k) on consumer hardware. X-axis: $\log _{10}$ USD of the full 9-job equivalent sweep per dataset per family, priced at the \emph {marginal electricity} of an M-series laptop: ${\sim }30$\,W sustained draw under inference $\times $ Polish residential electricity (\$0.20/kWh) $\approx $ \$0.006/hr. This is the cost the practitioner actually pays on top of a machine they already own for other work; it excludes the sunk capex of the laptop itself. The hardware bucket \texttt {M\_SERIES\_MAC} in \texttt {benchmark/code/evaluation/cost.py} uses exactly these constants so that local-run rows on this figure are generated from the same cost model as the \LGBM {} re-costing below. Y-axis: the per-dataset \WAPE {}\,/\,\WRMSSE {} of the best variant}{figure.4}{}}
